The functions `l2cap_register_user()` and `l2cap_unregister_user()` used `hci_dev_lock(hdev)` to protect access to `conn->users` and `conn->hchan`, while `l2cap_conn_del()` (which can run concurrently) used `mutex_lock(&conn->lock)` for the same purpose. This locking inconsistency created a race condition. Since these two locks are different, concurrent execution could corrupt the list and lead to use-after-free when `l2cap_conn_del()` modifies `conn->users` or sets `conn->hchan` to NULL while another thread is accessing them under the wrong lock.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
mutex_lock(&conn->lock);
